      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn the games so far, we have been learning in a clear order: first something happens, and then new knowledge builds on it. Let's review:\n- We saw that voice is not unique to our species, *Homo sapiens*. However, articulate speech is what sets us apart from our close relatives among the animals. In that text, we also learned that we can count using our body (<htcite0>).\n- Next, we saw that many animals lived before us and walked—or still walk—on Earth. What we all have in common is that we belong to a single genus, *Homo*. We also saw, at another stage of mathematical learning, that we can count using our feet (<htcite1>).\n- In the game before this one, we met DNA and learned that it passes on heredity. This lets us find relationships between ourselves and the ancient skeletons we find, known as fossils. Connecting that knowledge to earlier games, we also saw that we can add using our body (<htcite2>).\n\nBecause the changes that lead to a new species happen generation after generation, questions about earlier species naturally arise. So, let's begin with a reflection:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "80ae0cca-8a79-4be3-a359-f1dd8700110b",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Hands and music",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "cd150cb3-20bf-46c2-9327-10a39373e9da",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Walking and singing",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "8b947b40-dc98-4991-924d-d4356a0f55a8",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Exercising with numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Between us and them</b><br /><br />We are <i>Homo sapiens</i>, and we currently live all over the planet. However, we were not the first humans to spread across the planet.<br /><br /><i>Homo neanderthalensis</i> lived in Europe and parts of Asia and is very close to us (<htcite0>).<br /><br />Which animal would be the closest link between us and them?</div></div></center>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ab1dda1e-95c2-4087-8299-e851e58bdc6f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Humans",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_heidelbergensis",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### A common relative\n\nBetween <htdate0> and <htdate1>, another species of the genus *Homo*, very close to us, lived on Earth: *Homo heidelbergensis* (**Figure 1**) (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "75d196c9-1c1e-4106-bef5-ce8558d00d31",
                     "text": "Tattersall, I., & Schwartz, J. H.",
                     "page": "pp. 77",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2009",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "344b68b3-9002-4857-8e6f-21f2114ae462",
                     "text": "Dorey, Fran",
                     "page": "Homo heidelbergensis",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2021",
                        "month": "6",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ab1dda1e-95c2-4087-8299-e851e58bdc6f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Humans",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "-600000",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "-300000",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgHer\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgHer', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\" style=\"width: 42%\"/></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 1</b>: <i>Homo heidelbergensis</i> fossil. © Don Hitchcock. Originally at the <i>Western Australian Museum</i> (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4c4ac42b-d2a9-45e9-ab2c-3420e19be902",
                     "text": "Hitchcock, Don",
                     "page": "Homo heidelbergensis",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The period when *Homo heidelbergensis* lived has led researchers such as Fran Dorey, of the Australian Museum, to point out a possible relationship between us and other species of the genus *Homo* (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "344b68b3-9002-4857-8e6f-21f2114ae462",
                     "text": "Dorey, Fran",
                     "page": "Homo heidelbergensis",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2021",
                        "month": "6",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><ul class=\"citation\"> <li><i><i>Homo heidelbergensis</i> began to develop regional differences that eventually gave rise to two species of humans. European populations of <i>Homo heidelbergensis</i> evolved into <i>Homo neanderthalensis</i> (the Neanderthals), while a separate population of <i>Homo heidelbergensis</i> in Africa evolved into our own species, <i>Homo sapiens</i>.</i></li></ul></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Besides the time period, we would also like to point out the brain size, which is compatible with our own. This helps explain the close relationship among us, the Neanderthals (**Figure 2**), and this species (<htcite0>).\n\nBefore you move on to the math practice, we should note that not all the publications studied for History Tracers agree with this conclusion. Some researchers argue that we (*Homo sapiens*), *Homo neanderthalensis*, and *Homo heidelbergensis* share a common ancestor, instead of *Homo heidelbergensis* being that ancestor (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "75d196c9-1c1e-4106-bef5-ce8558d00d31",
                     "text": "Tattersall, I., & Schwartz, J. H.",
                     "page": "pp. 77",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2009",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7fa66cfd-dca8-4a17-bead-e79954ba684a",
                     "text": "Wood, B., & Collard, M.",
                     "page": "pp. 68",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1999",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td><i>Homo neanderthalensis</i></td><td><i>Homo sapiens</i></td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"imgNean\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgNean', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\" style=\"width: 20%\"/></td><td><img id=\"imgHs7\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgHs7', '0%')\" src=\"\" style=\"width: 100%;\"/></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: Fossil of <i>Homo neanderthalensis</i> on the left. © Don Hitchcock. Originally at the <i>Vienna Natural History Museum, Naturhistorisches Museum Wien</i> (<htcite0>). On the right, a poster of the human skeleton that describes <i>Homo sapiens</i>, at the Museo Nacional de Antropología in México (<htcite1>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4c4ac42b-d2a9-45e9-ab2c-3420e19be902",
                     "text": "Hitchcock, Don",
                     "page": "La Ferrassie Cave or Abri - a Neanderthal rock shelter",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0be2ea18-ab78-4cb1-b3be-1ff0ba4ade95",
                     "text": "Museo Nacional de Antropologia",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "### The Game\n\nEvolution happens because populations of living things keep having new generations — in other words, they are multiplying. So, to understand why we are here and what the next generations might be like, we need to master multiplication.\n\nIn this game, you will practice a calculation you already learned in earlier texts. To strengthen your knowledge, do the operation by moving your limbs (hands and feet), as suggested.\n\n#### Rules\n\nThe game shows a calculation for you to do with your body. To start, click the play button (<i class=\"fa-solid fa-play\" style=\"font-size:1em;color:#0f0;\" ></i>). If you want to repeat it and practice more, click it again.\n\nTo try a new calculation, click the arrow button (<i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-right\"></i>).\n\nYou can pick one multiplication table, or let the system choose one at random in the **Multiplication table** field.\n\nThe main goal of the game is to show that multiplication is repeated addition of the same quantity (<htcite0>). To do that, you will move a set number of limbs over and over, according to the multiplication table.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p style=\"text-align=center\"><center>&nbsp;<span id=\"nextVal\"></span>&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span id=\"topVal\"></span><br />×&nbsp;&nbsp;<span id=\"bottomVal\"></span><br />____________<br />&nbsp;&nbsp;<span id=\"resVal\"></span></center></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Multiplication to be performed with the body.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"speed-control\"><b>Multiplication Table:</b> <select id=\"mtValues\"><option value=\"-1\">Randomly</option></select> <label>Slow</label> <input type=\"range\" id=\"speedSlider\" min=\"600\" max=\"1600\" value=\"1100\" step=\"10\" style=\"direction: rtl;\"> <label>Fast</label>  <i class=\"fa-solid fa-play\" onclick=\"htExecuteMult();\" style=\"font-size:2em;color:#0f0;\" ></i> <i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-right\"  onclick=\"htNewLocalMultiplication();\" style=\"font-size:2em;\"></i></div> <div class=\"counters\"> <b>Current Number</b><span id=\"clapCounter\" class=\"counter\">: 0</span><input type=\"hidden\" id=\"handPosCounter\" /> <input type=\"hidden\" id=\"clapCount\" value=\"0\" /></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
